<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dunkeld</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dunkeld is a small town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tay">River Tay</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perthshire">Perthshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. In the 9th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenneth-macalpin">Kenneth MacAlpin</a> is said to have brought relics of St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a> there, and it became a leading church centre of the kingdom of Alba. Its lay abbot Crínán was the father of King Duncan I, so the royal line that ruled until 1286 is known as the House of Dunkeld. Its partly ruined medieval cathedral still stands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dunkeld.jpg" alt="Dunkeld" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diliff · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dunkeld_Cathedral_Exterior,_Dunkeld,_UK_-_Diliff.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
